{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% macro diagram(key) -%}
<figure class="diagram"><div class="canvas">{{ d[key][0]|safe }}</div><figcaption>{{ d[key][1] }}</figcaption></figure>
{%- endmacro %}
{% block main %}
<nav class="localnav" aria-label="Architecture sections">
  <div class="localnav-inner">
    <span class="localnav-title">Architecture</span>
    <div class="localnav-links">
      <a href="#context">Context</a>
      <a href="#containers">Containers</a>
      <a href="#principles">Principles</a>
      <a href="#report-run">Report run</a>
      <a href="#ask">Ask</a>
      <a href="#safety">Safety</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</nav>

<section class="hero arch-hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
  <h1>Simple parts.<br><span class="grad">Clear boundaries.</span></h1>
  <p class="lede">spendlens is a Python CLI that turns AWS APIs into SQL with Steampipe, runs your company's controls with
    Powerpipe, and keeps every result in a local history that reports, notifications and the AI assistant read.</p>
  <ul class="stack-strip" aria-label="Built with">
    <li>Python</li><li>Steampipe</li><li>Powerpipe</li><li>SQLite</li><li>Jinja2</li><li>sqlglot</li>
    <li>Anthropic SDK</li><li>OpenAI SDK</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section showcase arch" id="context">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">C4 · Level 1</p>
        <h2>Who uses it.<br>What it talks to.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
        <p>People and pipelines ask the cost questions; spendlens reads AWS, optionally asks an LLM, and posts summaries
          to chat. Every arrow into AWS is a read.</p>
        <ul class="chips"><li>Read-only towards AWS</li><li>No agents</li><li>No SaaS</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ diagram("c4_context") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase arch alt" id="containers">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">C4 · Level 2</p>
        <h2>What runs inside.<br>How data moves.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
        <p>Controls run through Powerpipe, cost questions go straight to Steampipe as bounded SQL, and every run lands
          in a SQLite history that the dashboard, notifications and the AI assistant read without touching AWS again.</p>
        <ul class="chips"><li>{{ stats.controls }} controls</li><li>One history</li><li>Pluggable</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ diagram("c4_container") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="principles">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Four design decisions.</h2>
    <p class="sub">The choices that keep spendlens small, predictable and easy to extend.</p>
    <div class="bento arch-bento">
      <article class="tile t-wide">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["list"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Controls via Powerpipe</p>
        <h3>Your standards, as SQL controls.</h3>
        <p>Tag, rightsizing and waste rules are controls in a bundled Powerpipe mod. Thresholds live in
          <code>finops.yaml</code> and are passed in as mod variables, so one file holds your company standards, and
          every control's SQL is checked offline against the AWS plugin's schema.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["chart"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Cost via direct SQL</p>
        <h3>Bounded queries.</h3>
        <p>Cost diff, trend, showback, anomalies and forecast query Steampipe's Cost Explorer tables directly, bounded
          to exactly the periods they need.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["clock"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">History as the hub</p>
        <h3>Collect once, read often.</h3>
        <p>Every run is saved to SQLite with its findings, cost rows, parameters and CI metadata. Reports and the
          assistant work offline from it.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile t-wide">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["puzzle"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Plugins everywhere</p>
        <h3>Built-ins use the same registry as yours.</h3>
        <p>Commands, Powerpipe mods, savings estimators, AI tools, doctor checks and dashboard tabs all register
          through one plugin API. A broken plugin is reported, never fatal.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase arch alt" id="report-run">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Sequence · Scheduled report</p>
        <h2>When the pipeline fires.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
        <p>The pipeline holds no AWS keys: it trades an OIDC token for a short-lived read-only role. Each step runs in
          its own process, so one failure never stops the rest, and history carries "what changed" to next week.</p>
        <ul class="chips"><li>OIDC, no stored keys</li><li>Isolated steps</li><li>Slack and Teams</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ diagram("seq_report") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase arch" id="ask">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Sequence · AI assistant</p>
        <h2>A question becomes<br>an answer.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
        <p>The model never touches a database. It asks for a query; spendlens checks it, runs it read-only and returns
          the rows. The answer cites the run it came from.</p>
        <ul class="chips"><li>Bedrock · Anthropic · OpenAI · Azure</li><li>Official SDKs</li><li>Audit trail</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ diagram("seq_ask") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase arch alt" id="ask-live">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Sequence · AI + Steampipe AWS plugin</p>
        <h2>Any AWS question,<br>read-only.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
        <p>With <code>--live</code> the assistant can answer from any of the AWS plugin's tables, not only cost:
          inventory, security groups, IAM, Lambda runtimes and more. It never calls AWS directly. It writes SQL,
          and Steampipe turns that SQL into read-only AWS API calls with your credentials.</p>
        <ul class="chips"><li>Hundreds of AWS tables</li><li>Your IAM role decides what it can see</li><li>Every query in the audit trail</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ diagram("seq_ask_live") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="agent-loop">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="t-eyebrow center">How the AI works</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Plan, query, check, answer.</h2>
    <p class="sub">The model is a planner, not an operator. It can only ask spendlens to run one of its tools, and every
      tool reads.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><h3>You ask</h3>
        <p>spendlens sends your question, the FinOps analyst instructions and the tool list to your AI provider:
          Bedrock, Anthropic, OpenAI, Azure OpenAI or any OpenAI-compatible server (Ollama, vLLM, GLM).</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>It discovers</h3>
        <p>It starts with your saved runs (<code>history_overview</code>). With <code>--live</code> it can also list
          the AWS plugin's tables and read their columns, so it learns the schema instead of guessing.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>It queries</h3>
        <p>It writes one <code>SELECT</code>. spendlens parses it, rejects anything that is not a single read, and
          runs it on a read-only connection with a timeout and a row cap.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Steampipe calls AWS</h3>
        <p>The AWS plugin maps the table to <code>Describe*</code>, <code>List*</code> or <code>Get*</code> calls in
          your configured regions, pages through the results and returns rows. Cost Explorer tables cost about
          $0.01 per request.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>It repeats</h3>
        <p>Rows go back to the model as data, never as instructions. It may refine the query or join another table,
          up to <code>llm.max_steps</code> tool calls (12 by default).</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>You check</h3>
        <p>The answer cites the SQL and numbers it used. <code>--show-tools</code> prints every call, and
          <code>--json</code> saves the full audit trail. Savings are proposals for you to review.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase ai" id="safety">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="t-eyebrow center">Safety</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Three read-only layers.<br>Zero writes.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Every query the AI assistant writes must pass the parser, then runs on a connection that cannot
      write: the read-only history, or with <code>--live</code>, a read-only Steampipe session.</p>
    <div class="layers">
      <article class="layer"><b>1</b><h3>SQL guard</h3>
        <p>The query is parsed with sqlglot. Only one read-only <code>SELECT</code> passes: no writes, DDL,
          <code>SELECT INTO</code>, multiple statements, <code>SET</code>, or functions such as
          <code>pg_read_file</code>.</p></article>
      <article class="layer"><b>2</b><h3>Read-only history</h3>
        <p>The SQLite history opens in read-only mode with <code>query_only</code> and an authorizer that denies
          everything except reads.</p></article>
      <article class="layer"><b>3</b><h3>Read-only sessions</h3>
        <p>Live Steampipe queries run in sessions with <code>default_transaction_read_only</code>, a statement
          timeout and a row cap.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="grid arch-more">
      <article class="card"><span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["key"]|safe }}</span><h3>Credentials</h3>
        <p>The AWS plugin uses the standard credential chain: the role assumed in your terminal,
          <code>AWS_PROFILE</code>, SSO, or OIDC in CI. spendlens stores no AWS keys.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["spark"]|safe }}</span><h3>Any LLM, native SDKs</h3>
        <p>Claude through the official Anthropic SDK (Anthropic API or Bedrock with your AWS role); OpenAI, Azure OpenAI
          and compatible servers through the official OpenAI SDK.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["flask"]|safe }}</span><h3>Tested at three layers</h3>
        <p>Unit tests; integration tests with the real SDKs and Powerpipe; system tests that run the CLI end to end
          through Steampipe against an AWS emulator.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap narrow" style="text-align:center">
    <h2 class="h2">See it on your own account.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="margin-bottom:28px">Try it on sample data first; then point it at a read-only role.</p>
    <div class="ctas"><a class="btn primary" href="{{ root }}docs/getting-started/">Get started</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ root }}docs/permissions/">IAM permissions ›</a></div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
